title: Holding a repository in memory
tags: Storage Repository
kind: guide
aliases: in-memory in_memory resident mlock memlock
summary: Loaded with --in-memory, a repository's store is read into RAM and locked there, so that no read waits on the disk; writes still go to the disk, one commit each.

```
mf repo load --in-memory <root>
```

or ''Load in memory'' in the [[repos panel]]'s load form, `repos:load in-memory` from
the keyboard, `{"root": …, "in_memory": true}` for [[POST /repos/load]] — and, for a
repository the daemon loads at startup, `in-memory = true` in its `[[load]]` entry
([[Daemon configuration]]).

The load reads the whole store into memory — its phase `memory` counts the bytes —
then the repository works as any other. Nothing else changes:

* ''Writes'' are the store's own: each request commits to the file on disk, with its `fsync`. A crash loses nothing that was answered.
* ''Reads'' find every page already in RAM: none waits on the disk, not the first query after a boot, nor one whose pages the kernel would have evicted under memory pressure. A write finds there what it has to change, too.
* What a write adds to the store is held in its turn, when the next write starts.
* The watcher, the tasks, the log: as usual. Unloading lets go of the memory.

!! Locked, or only read in

To stay in memory, the pages are ''locked'' there (`mlock`), out of the kernel's
reach. A process may lock only so much: the ''memory-lock limit'' (`RLIMIT_MEMLOCK`),
often 8 MiB for a user — far below a real store. When the store is larger, it is
read in but not locked: it starts warm, and the kernel may evict some of it later,
as for an ordinary load. The daemon says so — in [[GET /repos]], in its
diagnostics, on the row of the [[repos panel]] (''in memory, not locked'', the reason
in its tooltip), and `mf repo load --in-memory` prints it as a warning — with the
limit and the store's size.

To lock it, raise the limit for the daemon: `LimitMEMLOCK=infinity` (or a size) in its
systemd unit, or a `memlock` line in `/etc/security/limits.conf` for a daemon started
from a session. The daemon raises its own soft limit to the hard one; it cannot go
further. Several repositories in memory share the limit. Locked or not, the memory
taken is the store's size: hold in memory only what fits.

`GET /repos` lists, for a repository loaded so, `in_memory: {bytes, pinned, reason}`
— `null` for one loaded the ordinary way.

!! What it costs, what it buys

The load reads the whole store: about a second more per 300 MiB on the development
machine (a 289 MiB store, 50 000 files: 1.0 to 2.3 s against 0.3 s). The memory is
the store's size, for as long as it is loaded.

What it buys is the absence of a ''cold'' read — the first one after a boot, or after
the kernel evicted the pages. There, the measure says nothing yet: in the development
container (fuse-overlayfs, a file the daemon keeps mapped) the cache cannot be made
cold, and a query that reads a few partitions took 10 to 30 ms either way, warm or not
(October 2026). A query reads a small part of the store, a reconcile or a large sort
more; the gain is largest where the store is read wide and the disk is slow.

!! Asking for it on a loaded repository

A repository already loaded the ordinary way is refused (`409`): its load would read
nothing in. Unload it, then load it in memory. Loading in memory a repository already
held in memory is the ordinary idempotent load.

!! Why one store, and not a copy in memory

LMDB reads through a memory map: a page in the page cache ''is'' in RAM, and reading
it costs the same as reading a copy made in memory. A second store, in a `tmpfs`,
with every write applied to both, would make no read faster — and would double each
write and give the two copies a way to disagree. What was missing is that the pages
''be'' there: read in at load, and kept. The same store, read in and locked, is all of
it (user decision, October 2026).

This answers, on demand, the cold-cache question of [[Storage open questions]]: an
ordinary load still reads nothing at open.
